--TEST--
An ordering of two objects throws a `LogicError` when their run-time classes differ, before `compareTo` runs, whether the operands are typed at an interface or at a parent class — `rule:classes/comparable-is-same-class-only`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Price implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public int $cents) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        return $this->cents <=> $other->cents;
    }
}

class Label implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public string $text) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        return 0;
    }
}

class SalePrice extends Price {
    public function constructor(int $cents, public string $reason) {
        parent::constructor($cents);
    }

    // The parameter stays `Price`, as the parent's is.
    public function compareTo(Price $other): int {
        echo "SalePrice::compareTo ran\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

class Shop {
    public static function item(bool $price): Comparable {
        if ($price) {
            return new Price(100);
        }
        return new Label("tea");
    }
}

// Two objects of the same class compare as `compareTo` says.
Price $cheap = new Price(100);
Price $dear = new Price(250);
echo ($cheap < $dear) ? "less" : "not less", "\n";

// The operands are typed `Comparable`, and they are a `Price` and a `Label`.
try {
    echo (Shop::item(true) < Shop::item(false)) ? "less" : "not less", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "1: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// Both operands are typed `Price`, and the left one is a `SalePrice`.
Price $sale = new SalePrice(80, "summer");
try {
    echo $sale <=> $cheap, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "2: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
// covers: lang:classes/comparable
--EXPECT--
less
1: `<` compares two objects of different classes. Both objects must have the same class.
2: `<=>` compares two objects of different classes. Both objects must have the same class.
